**Step‑by‑step derivation**

For a non‑relativistic particle of mass \(m\) the chemical potential obtained in part (1) is  

\[
\mu = mc^{2}+k_{\!B}T\ln\!\big(n\,\lambda^{3}\big),\qquad   
\lambda\equiv\frac{h}{\sqrt{2\pi mk_{\!B}T}} .
\tag{1}
\]

The reaction  

\[
\mathrm{D}+\gamma \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{p}+\mathrm{n}
\]

is at chemical equilibrium, i.e.  

\[
\mu _{D}+ \underbrace{\mu _{\gamma}}_{=0}= \mu _{p}+\mu _{n}
\;\Longrightarrow\; \mu _{D}= \mu _{p}+ \mu _{n}.
\tag{2}
\]

Insert Eq. (1) for the three massive particles (the photon does not appear because
its chemical potential is zero):

\[
\begin{aligned}
& m_{D}c^{2}+k_{\!B}T\ln\!\big(n_{D}\lambda_{D}^{3}\big) \\
&\quad=
\Big(m_{p}c^{2}+k_{\!B}T\ln\!\big(n_{p}\lambda_{p}^{3}\big)\Big)
+\Big(m_{n}c^{2}+k_{\!B}T\ln\!\big(n_{n}\lambda_{n}^{3}\big)\Big).
\end{aligned}
\tag{3}
\]

Using the definition of the binding energy  

\[
m_{D}c^{2}=m_{p}c^{2}+m_{n}c^{2}-B_{D},
\tag{4}
\]

the rest‑energy terms cancel, leaving only the logarithmic terms:

\[
k_{\!B}T\Big[\ln\!\big(n_{D}\lambda_{D}^{3}\big)
          -\ln\!\big(n_{p}\lambda_{p}^{3}\big)
          -\ln\!\big(n_{n}\lambda_{n}^{3}\big)\Big]
= B_{D}.
\]

Exponentiating gives the compact equilibrium relation

\[
\boxed{%
n_{D}\,\lambda_{D}^{3}=e^{\,B_{D}/(k_{\!B}T)}\;
      n_{p}\,\lambda_{p}^{3}\;n_{n}\,\lambda_{n}^{3}}.
\tag{5}
\]

Solving for the desired ratio yields

\[
\frac{n_{D}}{n_{p}n_{n}}
   = e^{\,B_{D}/(k_{\!B}T)}\;
     \frac{\lambda_{p}^{3}\,\lambda_{n}^{3}}{\lambda_{D}^{3}} .
\tag{6}
\]

---

### Expressing the result solely with constants

Since  

\[
\lambda_{i}=\frac{h}{\sqrt{2\pi m_{i}k_{\!B}T}},
\qquad(i=D,p,n),
\]

the product of three de‑Broglie wavelengths can be written explicitly:

\[
\frac{\lambda_{p}^{3}\lambda_{n}^{3}}{\lambda_{D}^{3}}
   =\frac{h^{3}}{(2\pi)^{3/2}}
     \left(\frac{m_{D}}{m_{p}m_{n}}\right)^{3/2}
     \frac{1}{(k_{\!B}T)^{3/2}} .
\tag{7}
\]

Using \(h=2\pi\hbar\) one may also rewrite it as  

\[
\frac{\lambda_{p}^{3}\lambda_{n}^{3}}{\lambda_{D}^{3}}
   =\left(\frac{2\pi\hbar^{2}}{k_{\!B}T}\right)^{3/2}
     \left(\frac{m_{D}}{m_{p}m_{n}}\right)^{3/2}.
\tag{8}
\]

---

## Final answer  

\[
\boxed{
\displaystyle
\frac{n_{D}}{n_{p}n_{n}}
= e^{\,B_{D}/(k_{\!B}T)}
   \,\frac{\lambda_{p}^{3}\,\lambda_{n}^{3}}{\lambda_{D}^{3}}
= e^{\,B_{D}/(k_{\!B}T)}\,
   \frac{h^{3}}{(2\pi)^{3/2}}
   \left(\frac{m_{D}}{m_{p}m_{n}}\right)^{\!3/2}
   \frac{1}{(k_{\!B}T)^{3/2}}
}
\]

or, equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
\displaystyle
\frac{n_{D}}{n_{p}n_{n}}
= e^{\,B_{D}/(k_{\!B}T)}
   \left(\frac{2\pi\hbar^{2}}{k_{\!B}T}\right)^{3/2}
   \left(\frac{m_{D}}{m_{p}m_{n}}\right)^{3/2}
}.
\]

These formulas give the equilibrium number‑density ratio of deuterium,
protons and neutrons in terms of the temperature \(T\), the deuteron binding
energy \(B_{D}\), the particle masses, Planck’s constant \(h\) (or \(\hbar\)),
and Boltzmann’s constant \(k_{\!B}\).
